.Dd September 25, 2026
.Dt RESTORE_MINIXFS 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm restore_minixfs
.Nd restore a dump into a MINIX file system
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Fl t
.Op Fl c
.Fl f Ar file
.Nm
.Fl r
.Op Fl cNv
.Op Fl M Ar size : Ns Ar heads : Ns Ar side
.Op Fl o Ar uid : Ns Ar gid
.Op Fl s Ar symtable
.Fl f Ar file
.Ar image
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
reads a dump in the format of BSD
.Xr dump 8 :
those of
.Xr dump_minixfs 8 ,
those of
.Nx
and
.Fx
of UFS1 and UFS2, those of the dump of Linux of ext2, ext3 and ext4, and
those of 4.2BSD and 4.3BSD of their file system and of the older one,
in either byte order.
With
.Fl r
it writes the dump into the MINIX file system in
.Ar image ,
of any version; see
.Xr minixfs 5 .
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl t
List the names of the files in the dump, each after its inode number in
the dump, as
.Xr restore 8
does.
The dates, the level and the label of the dump come first.
.It Fl r
Restore the dump into the root directory of
.Ar image :
a full dump into an empty file system, then each dump of a higher level
in the order they were made.
Each directory of the dump comes to hold what the dump says it holds:
names that are gone are removed and new ones made, a file whose last
name went away is freed, and files and directories take their modes,
owners and times from the dump.
.It Fl c
Read the directories as those of V7, of 16-byte entries, as
.Xr restore 8
does with
.Fl c .
Those of a dump of a file system older than 4.2BSD are always read so.
.It Fl f Ar file
The dump;
.Ql -
is standard input.
.It Fl M Ar size : Ns Ar heads : Ns Ar side
Write to an image that holds the file system in the tracks of one side
of a disk only, as
.Xr minixfs 1
reads it.
.It Fl N
Check everything, and write nothing.
.It Fl o Ar uid : Ns Ar gid
Make every file owned by
.Ar uid
and
.Ar gid ,
numbers both.
.It Fl s Ar symtable
The file where
.Fl r
keeps which inode of the image each inode of the dump went to, for the
next dump of a higher level, and the date of the dump it restored.
The default is
.Pa restoresymtable ,
as that of
.Xr restore 8 .
.It Fl v
Name each directory and file as it is restored.
.El
.Pp
A dump holds its directories before its other files.
Having read them,
.Fl r
checks the names against the length the file system takes, and the
number of inodes needed against those free, and writes nothing if
anything is wrong.
From a file, rather than standard input, it also reads through the other
files first and checks their owners, device numbers and sizes;
from standard input, a file that does not fit is left out when it comes,
with its names, and the rest goes in.
Device numbers are taken as major \(mu 256 + minor, and have to be below
65536.
.Pp
A dump of a higher level has to follow the dump restored last: its date
of the dump before it has to be the date in
.Ar symtable .
A restore that fails, or a dump that ends too soon, leaves what was
written in order, and a
.Ar symtable
by which the same dump follows again, once what failed is put right.
.Pp
The file system has to be marked clean, and not to have the flex
directories of Minix-vmd.
While it is written the clean mark is away, and it comes back once the
restore is on the disk.
A restore cut short, or one in which an operation on the image failed
other than for a lack of room or a limit of the file system, such as an
error of the device, leaves it away: check the file system with
.Nm fsck_minixfs Fl y
then.
A dump that ends too soon or is damaged, or a file system that runs out
of room, does not, as what was written is left in order.
.Pp
Extended attributes, file flags and sockets have no place in a MINIX
file system, and are left out with a warning.
So are the names of files that the dump does not hold, as in a dump of
a part of a file system.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits with 0 on success, 1 if anything failed, and 2 for a usage error.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Restore a full dump and one of level 1 made after it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# newfs_minixfs -V 3 -s 65536 usr.img
# restore_minixfs -r -f /backup/usr.0 usr.img
# restore_minixfs -r -f /backup/usr.1 usr.img
# rm restoresymtable
.Ed
.Pp
Put a dump of an ext2 file system of Linux into a V2 file system, owned
by root:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# restore_minixfs -r -o 0:0 -f home.dump home.img
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr minixfs 1 ,
.Xr minixfs 5 ,
.Xr dump 8 ,
.Xr dump_minixfs 8 ,
.Xr fsck_minixfs 8 ,
.Xr newfs_minixfs 8 ,
.Xr restore 8
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Shoichi Fukusaka
.Sh BUGS
Compressed dumps of Linux and dumps on more than one volume are not
read.
